\fs22 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Additional work on MCP (Model Context Protocol) over MQTT, physical AI agents on AWS IoT Core combining MCP + MQTT, and traditional multi-robot/AGV swarm coordination via MQTT (navigation, obstacle sharing, trajectory updates).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf2 How it maps to \'93swarm governance\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs22 \cf2 MQTT\'92s built-in primitives (hierarchical topics, retained messages for discovery/state, shared subscriptions for load balancing, Last Will for presence/failure detection, topic ACLs for authorization) are being treated as native coordination/governance mechanisms. Agents register, discover capabilities, form ad-hoc workflows, and handle failures in a decentralized way \'97 closer to swarm self-organization than classic centralized orchestrators.\
This is especially practical for 
\f0\b hybrid physical + software agent swarms
\f1\b0  (IoT fleets, robotics, edge devices) where low overhead, unreliable networks, and massive scale matter. Pure software multi-agent \'93governance\'94 (voting, consensus, market mechanisms, safety interlocks) is still more often handled at the application layer, with MQTT (or similar) as the messaging fabric underneath.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Bottom line
\f1\b0 : MQTT is not just theoretical for agentic AI \'97 major MQTT platforms (EMQX, HiveMQ), open specs (A2A over MQTT, MCP over MQTT), research systems, and edge/IoT deployments are using it right now for multi-agent coordination and swarm-style governance, particularly where agents are tied to devices or need lightweight, event-driven, scalable messaging.\
\
